What do the following expressions have in common? \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  a thorn in the flesh \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  how are the mighty fallen \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  the skin of my teeth \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  from strength to strength \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  reap the whirlwind \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  eat, drink, and be merry \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  see eye to eye \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  fell flat on his face \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  all things to all men \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  suffer fools gladly \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  salt of the earth \par
\par
\f1\u9632?\f0  in the twinkling of an eye \par
\par
The answer is that they are all found in the English translation of the Bible formerly known as the Authorised Version, now more generally referred to as the King James Bible (KJB). This translation was first published in 1611, and the four-hundredth anniversary of its appearance is being celebrated in 2011 in a variety of events, publications and broadcasts. There are two main reasons for these celebrations. The first is that the literary style and rhythms of the KJB \f2\emdash  experienced particularly when it is read aloud \emdash  make it one of the most beautiful and memorable works of prose in English. The second is because of its vast religious, cultural and historical influence. For over three centuries, it was the only version of the Bible read and heard across the English-speaking world. Even after other translations began to be published from the late-nineteenth century onwards, it held its place in the affection of readers, particularly in the USA where it continued to be the best-selling version of the Bible until 1988. \par
\par
The history of the book \par
\par
The story of how it came into existence is a fascinating one. It was by no means the first translation of the Bible into English, and indeed may be seen instead as the culmination of a century of intensive translation activity. Under the impetus of the Renaissance and the Protestant Reformation in the sixteenth century, the Bible had begun to be translated from its original languages of Hebrew (Old Testament) and Greek (New Testament) into the vernacular languages of Western Europe. \par
\par
This represented a fundamental challenge to the authority of the Roman Catholic Church. For over one thousand years the official Bible of the Church was the Latin translation made by St Jerome in the late fourth century, known as The Vulgate \emdash  meaning the Bible in the vulgar tongue, which at that time was Latin. In the sixteenth century the Catholic Church bitterly opposed any suggestion that the Scriptures should be made available to ordinary readers in their own language. Protestant reformers, however, regarded the Bible, not the Church, as the ultimate source of religious authority. Martin Luther published his famous translation of the New Testament into German in 1522, and followed it with a complete Bible 12 years later. \par
\par
Tyndale and earlier English versions \par
\par
Luther\rquote s example inspired a young English scholar, William Tyndale, to conceive of making a similar translation. This was an exceedingly dangerous thing to do, because in England at that time the making of any unauthorised translation of the Bible was against the law, and punishable by death. Tyndale therefore went to Germany, and his New Testament \emdash  the first to be printed in English, and the first in English to have been translated directly from the Greek \emdash  was printed there in 1526. Large numbers were smuggled into England, to the fury of the authorities. He began translating the Old Testament from the Hebrew, but was tracked down, captured and put to death as a heretic in 1536, before he could complete his work. \par
\par
Picture \par
\par
Tyndale was a supremely gifted translator. His clear, vigorous English rendering influenced all subsequent translations, and indeed his wording was often simply taken over by them. Complete Bibles incorporating his work were published in 1535, 1536 and 1539, but these were large and expensive volumes, for use only in churches. \par
\par
Then, in 1560, a new translation was published by a group of English Protestant scholars in exile in Geneva. The \lquote Geneva Bible\rquote  as it was known became extremely popular, partly because it was published in smaller formats that were cheap to buy, but also because it provided marginal notes interpreting the text from a strongly Protestant viewpoint. It was the first Bible in English to divide chapters into numbered verses \emdash  thus giving rise to the phrase \lquote chapter and verse\rquote . The Geneva Bible, however, was disapproved of by conservative bishops in the Church of England, who felt that it undermined their authority. They produced their own \lquote Bishops\rquote  Bible\rquote  in 1568, but this failed to supplant the Geneva Bible, over 80 editions of which were published between 1568 and 1611. \par
\par
The Hampton Court Conference \par
\par
James VI of Scotland, who ascended to the throne of England in 1603 as James I, was also hostile to the Geneva Bible, regarding its notes as politically dangerous and calling into question royal and ecclesiastical authority. At a great conference of church dignitaries which met with him at Hampton Court in 1604, it was resolved that a new translation should be commissioned, \lquote to be set out and printed without any marginal notes, and\'85 to be used in all Churches of England in time of divine service\rquote . Published in 1611, this was the Bible that would become famous as the \lquote Authorised Version\rquote  or the \lquote King James Bible\rquote . \par
\par
The work was carried out by a team of 54 leading scholars, divided into six groups. Their brief was quite explicit. They were not to start from scratch, but were to base themselves on the text of the Bishops\rquote  Bible, only revising when it seemed necessary to render more accurately the Hebrew or Greek. While faithfulness to the original text was of paramount importance, it is clear that great care was also taken to ensure that the wording of the new translation would sound graceful and dignified when read aloud. Often this effect was achieved by making quite small changes, as we can see by comparing the KJB wording of Matthew 11:28 with that in the Bishops\rquote  Bible: \par
\par
Come unto me all ye that labour sore, and are laden, and I will ease you. (Bishops\rquote  Bible, 1568) \par
\par
Come unto me all ye that labour, and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest. (KJB, 1611) \par
\par
The tiny changes made by the KJB translators do not change the meaning, but they make the sentence rhythmically much more beautiful and therefore memorable. Another famous example is the \lquote still small voice\rquote  heard by Elijah in 1 Kings 19:12. In the Bishops\rquote  Bible this had been a \lquote small still voice\rquote , but the KJB translators recognised that by gradually increasing the length of the vowels (from the short \lquote i\rquote  to the longer \lquote a\rquote ), the words and meaning come together more satisfyingly. \par
\par
The preparation of the KJB \emdash  between 1604 and 1611 \emdash  coincided with the production of some of Shakespeare\rquote s greatest plays, and, as with Shakespeare, many of its phrases and idioms have been absorbed into everyday English. The examples quoted at the beginning of this article are included by David Crystal in his study of how the KJB has shaped the English language. He lists some 257 idiomatic or quasi-proverbial expressions which derive from the KJB \emdash  far more than from any other single source. Only Shakespeare comes close, but although he coined many more new words, fewer than one hundred idiomatic expressions can be specifically attributed to him. \par
\par
Literary influence \par
\par
The contribution of idioms is, of course, only a small part of the influence of the Bible in English, and indeed almost by definition an idiom becomes so thoroughly embedded in the language that people forget its connection with the source text. Of far greater importance for students of English literature are the ways in which words, passages and stories from the Bible are absorbed and put to imaginative purposes by creative writers. Perhaps the greatest of all literary works to have been influenced by the KJB is John Milton\rquote s epic poem, Paradise Lost (1667) which retells the biblical story of the fall and redemption of Adam and Eve, as recounted in the first three chapters of Genesis. Milton sometimes stays very close to the KJB text, as in the description of the creation in Book 7, but elsewhere he enlarges on the biblical source, humanising the characters and providing them with plausible reasons for their actions. \par
\par
John Bunyan\rquote s famous allegory of the Christian life, The Pilgrim\rquote s Progress (1678), is also saturated with references and allusions to the Bible, as, for example, in the names of many of the places through which Christian and his companions Faithful and Hopeful travel. \lquote Vanity Fair\rquote  (later to become the title of a novel by Thackeray) is inspired by Ecclesiastes 1:2: \lquote Vanity of vanities, saith the preacher, vanity of vanities; all is vanity\rquote . The \lquote Valley of the Shadow of Death\rquote  is straight out of Psalm 23:4: \lquote Yea, though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil: for thou art with me\rquote . The description of the Celestial City is drawn from the account in Revelation of the heavenly Jerusalem, its streets paved with gold (21:21), its inhabitants clothed in white robes (7:9), playing harps and singing \lquote Blessing, and honour, and glory, and power, be unto him that sitteth upon the throne, and unto the Lamb for ever and ever\rquote  (5:13). \par
\par
Milton and Bunyan were among the first generation of writers to have been brought up on the KJB. As time went on, it became so universally familiar and its words and images were etched so deeply into people\rquote s minds that writers were able to allude to it in increasingly rich and complex and unexpected ways. Even such an iconoclastic and irreverent a poet as Byron includes many references to the Bible in his works (he claimed to have read the Old Testament through with delight before he was eight years old). His highly controversial verse drama Cain (1821) was a rewriting of the biblical story of Cain, the jealous murderer of his brother Abel (Genesis 4:1\endash 15). Most of the poems in Hebrew Melodies (1815) were based on some figure or incident from the Old Testament. The most widely anthologised of these, \lquote The Destruction of Sennacherib\rquote  (\lquote The Assyrian came down like the wolf on the fold\rquote ), is an extraordinarily vivid rendering of a verse from 2 Kings chapter 19: \lquote And it came to pass that night, that the angel of the LORD went out, and smote in the camp of the Assyrians an hundred fourscore and five thousand: and when they arose early in the morning, behold, they were all dead corpses\rquote : \par
\par
For the Angel of Death spread his wings on the blast, \par
And breathed in the face of the foe as he passed; \par
And the eyes of the sleepers waxed deadly and chill, \par
And their hearts but once heaved, and for ever grew still! \par
\par
Victorian and contemporary literature \par
\par
The Bible is present everywhere in Victorian literature. To give just one example, near the beginning of Jane Eyre (1847) the young heroine lists her favourite parts of the Bible: \lquote I like Revelations and the book of Daniel, and Genesis and Samuel, and a little bit of Exodus, and some parts of Kings and Chronicles, and Job and Jonah\rquote . This is not a random list, but can be seen to relate to the larger concerns of the novel. Daniel, famously, stood up for his beliefs even though he was thrown into a den of lions; Job and Jonah suffered unjustly; the book of Revelation prophesies that an apocalyptic day of judgement is coming. \par
\par
The KJB has continued to exert a powerful influence on modern writers, none more so than the Nobel prize-winning novelist Toni Morrison. A book from the Old Testament provides the title for one of her novels, Song of Solomon (1977), and some words from verses in it \emdash  \lquote I am my beloved\rquote s, and my beloved is mine\rquote  (Song of Solomon 6:3) \emdash  are alluded to in the title of another of her novels, Beloved (1987), and are echoed several times at the opening and ending of chapters, defining mother\endash daughter\endash sister relationships in the novel. \par
\par
The KJB no longer holds the dominant position in Anglophone culture that it once did. Many other translations of the Bible are now available, and these, of course, have been able to incorporate significant advances in knowledge about the ancient languages and discoveries of manuscripts on which the text of the Bible is based, and they are presented in contemporary English. It is safe to say, however, that none of these modern translations, however excellent they may be, will have the influence that the KJB has had. Itself one of the greatest works of English literature, its impact on the development of English language and literature over four centuries has been enormous and is unparalleled. Every student of English needs to become acquainted with the King James Bible. \par
\par
